Numerical regression tests for the FreeType gray rasterizer cell cache
=====================================================================

`target.cpp` (built by `run.sh target`) regression-tests the band replay
behaviour of the stand-alone smooth rasterizer (`src/smooth/ftgrays.c`,
`STANDALONE_`).  Nothing here loads a font or needs a graphical front end:
the support build compiles the *same* unchanged `ftgrays.c` twice, once
with the default 16 KiB render pool (`ft_grays_raster`) and once with a
2 KiB pool (`ft_grays_raster_small`), and the test renders raw `FT_Outline`
data (26.6 coordinates, hand-written points/tags/contours) through those
two exported `FT_Raster_Funcs` objects.

The purpose is to protect the cell cache while it is being reworked: when
the pool is too small and the rasterizer bisects a row range into more
horizontal bands, the same outline must still cover exactly the same
pixels, with no missing first band row, no missing curve segment and no
lost winding information.

The three groups are registered with `CASE` and use `CHECK` for every
assertion; `runner.cpp` supplies `main`, `verify.awk` rejects an incomplete
run, and `run.sh` additionally rebuilds two isolated semantic mutations
(`drop_band`, which shifts each band's `min_ey` by one, and `lose_parity`,
which ignores `FT_OUTLINE_EVEN_ODD_FILL`) and requires the same tests to
reject them by pixel content.

Reference error bounds
----------------------

The expected values come from `reference_render()` in `target.cpp`.  It
is independent of FreeType's own decomposition, flattening, winding and
coverage code:

* The outline is decomposed from the raw tags into line / quadratic /
  cubic segments in double precision, including the implicit starts
  (first point a conic control with the last point on-curve, or both end
  points conic, where the start is their midpoint).
* Curves are flattened adaptively with an explicit geometric bound:
  quadratic while the control lies within `2 * 5e-4 px` of the chord,
  cubic while both controls lie within `5e-4 px` of the chord (the
  flattened curve then stays well within that distance of the chords).
* For each scanline the x extent of the filled set is integrated exactly
  in y.  The row is cut into slabs at every vertex height, at every y
  where an edge crosses a pixel column boundary, and at every edge/edge
  crossing, so the crossing order is constant inside a slab; there each
  crossing moves linearly in y and the covered length of a pixel is
  linear in y, which makes the trapezoid rule exact.
* Winding (non-zero) and parity (even-odd) are evaluated from the ordered
  crossings, so nested, reversed and touching subcontours are handled
  topologically; unsigned absolute areas are never summed, which would
  be wrong for inner rings and touching subcontours.

The geometric bound is `5e-4 px`.  The `curves` group contains a
reference self-check: it re-flattens a curved fixture at a 100x tighter
bound (`5e-6 px`) and requires the two reference images to agree to within
0.5 gray level everywhere.  Measured over all shipped curve fixtures the
disagreement is at most 0.12 gray level, so the reference's own error is
far below half a gray level.  The slab integral was also cross-checked
against an independent 64x64 sub-pixel sampling of the same flattened
polygons; on the shipped fixtures the two agree to within the sampling
error (typically 0.1-0.2 gray level).

Tolerances applied by the tests:

* straight edges: 1 gray level per pixel;
* fixtures containing curves: 4 gray levels per pixel.

The per-pixel tolerance is chosen per pixel: pixels within 1.5 px of
a curve are allowed the curve tolerance; all others get the straight
tolerance.

Note that the two-pool comparison alone is *not* the test.  Both pools
compile the same code, so they can miss a fill-rule bug together.  The
comparison against the independent reference is what makes the assertions
meaningful; the pool comparison only pins down the banding behaviour.

Fixtures
--------

`bands`: a scene that genuinely exceeds both cell pools.

* 26 narrow (about 1.2 px wide) vertical strips span most of the 96-row
  height.  Each strip is two live cell columns in every row, and the
  strips together are far more cells than the small pool can hold.
* a tall continuous contour with gentle cubic S sides, and 12 tall
  low-curvature conic lenses, add cells without adding self-intersections.
* The same outline is rendered at identity, with an integer translation,
  with a negative fractional translation, and under even-odd filling.

Band scheduling is measured, not assumed: for this 96-row canvas the
default pool re-bisects 10 times and the small pool 75 times (instrumented
only temporarily; `ftgrays.c` is not modified).  Strip ends sit on
initial band edges and on bisection heights, and the lenses' y extrema
straddle the small-pool band edges (2, 4, 5, 6, 10, 12, 24, 48) on both
sides of the bisection heights, so a dropped or shifted band shows up as
a missing strip end or a cut lens apex.  The straight strips are also
checked against an independent per-pixel rectangle-intersection area
(`want = (hi - lo) * 255`) rather than only against the reference
rasterizer.

`curves`: quadratic, cubic and conic contours that cross bands and
contain in-band y extrema and inflections; implicit quadratic starts
(both end points conic, and first point conic / last point on-curve);
same-direction and reversed inner rings; and touching-but-not-overlapping
subcontours.  Every case runs under non-zero and even-odd.  A separate
set of integral, axis-aligned rectangles is emitted twice and checked
exactly: filled under non-zero, cancelled under even-odd.  The remaining
fixtures are simple non-self-intersecting outlines with at least 2 px
between inner and outer boundaries.

Note on curve fixtures: the shipped production rasterizer flattens Beziers
with a fixed deviation criterion (the conic is split until its control
deviates by at most a quarter pixel, the cubic until both control
expressions are within half a pixel), so strongly curved shapes
legitimately deviate from the reference by more than 4 gray levels.  The
curve fixtures are therefore deliberately low-curvature (measured against
the reference at 0.7-3.0 gray levels across the six fixtures), which is
the tightest form of the 4-gray bound that the rasterizer can meet without
changing its algorithm.  This is a real property of the renderer, not a
relaxed test: no tolerance here is wide enough to hide a missing row,
because a missing row moves whole pixels by up to 255 gray levels.

`layouts`: the same outlines are rendered both into an `FT_Bitmap` and
through the `DIRECT` gray_spans callback.  The callback's coordinates are
interpreted in a bottom-up pixel space and the spans are reassembled into
one image that must equal the bitmap; a callback may deliver several
spans at once and the code handles that.  DIRECT is exercised with non-zero
clip boxes, with a clip box whose origin is negative (the rectangle
crossing both axes produces genuinely negative span x and y), and with
left/right-clipped and empty clips.  Bitmaps are tested with positive and
negative pitch plus per-row padding and guard sentinels; only the
`width x rows` valid area is compared, and padding never counts toward
area.  After each render the outline and its points/tags/contours are
checked to be byte-identical to a snapshot.  Outlines and pools are
interleaved, an empty DIRECT call is issued between renders, and every
render is repeated and compared to the first, so no span or coverage
data may carry over between calls.

Not covered
-----------

* cubic control points as the very first outline point (invalid for
  `FT_Outline_Decompose`, so the fixtures start with an on-curve or a
  conic point);
* strongly curved Beziers whose error against the reference exceeds the
  straight per-pixel tolerance without an algorithmic change (see above);
* self-intersecting outlines and overlapping contours whose edges cross
  within a single sub-pixel; the reference evaluates topology from the
  crossing order, but the shipped fixtures deliberately keep the inner
  and outer boundaries at least 2 px apart, and even-odd area on
  overlapping sub-pixel boundaries is not asserted;
* monochrome (`FT_RASTER_FLAG_AA` off) rendering, which the smooth
  rasterizer rejects;
* drop-out handling, hinting, colour, and any glyph/format loading;
* the `FT_RASTER_FLAG_CLIP` double meaning outside `DIRECT` (the
  rasterizer ignores it and clips by the bitmap size);
* performance, threading and render-pool reuse across raster objects
  (stand-alone mode uses a static raster object).
